  "id": "COMPOUND:14494 - PROTEIN:5807",
  "verbalization": "N-methylscopolamine appears to exert its biological effects through a series of interconnected pathways that ultimately target prostate cells. The drug is directly linked to the H(+)/Cl(-) exchange transporter 3, which is a target of Teyin, and Teyin further targets prostate cells. This suggests a potential mechanism involving the modulation of ion transport in prostate cells, possibly through the activation of Teyin, which in turn influences cellular processes in the prostate. Additionally, N-methylscopolamine is also associated with Prelamin-A/C, which is a target of Michaolide J, and Michaolide J also targets prostate cells. While the exact mechanism by which Prelamin-A/C influences prostate cells remains unclear, the shared targeting of prostate cells across multiple pathways indicates a possible role in prostate-specific cellular functions. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which N-methylscopolamine may affect prostate cells, though further investigation is needed to elucidate the precise molecular interactions involved.",
